"SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.16245v1)\nciteturn65view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.16245v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 298\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background L18:   4. cite8†3 System Overview L19:   5. cite9†4 Evaluation L20:     1. cite10†4.1 Evaluation Setup L21:     2. cite11†4.2 Interference Impact on Multi-core Platforms L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Interference Mitigation Techniques L23:   6. cite13†5 Discussion and Future Directions L24:   7. cite14†6 Related Work L25:   8. cite15†7 Conclusion L26:   9. cite16†References L27: \nL28: cite17†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L29: \nL30: arXiv:2501.16245v1 [cs.DC] 27 Jan 2025\nL31: GPUs\nL32:     Graphics Processing Units\nL33: TPUs\nL34:     Tensor Processing Units\nL35: NPUs\nL36:     Neural Processing Units\nL37: CPUs\nL38:     Central Processing Units\nL39: FPGAs\nL40:     Field-Programmable Gate Arrays\nL41: SWaP-C\nL42:     Size, Weight, Power, and Cost\nL43: MCSs\nL44:     Mixed Criticality Systems\nL45: SPH\nL46:     Static Partitioning Hypervisors\nL47: LLC\nL48:     Last-Level Cache\nL49: WCET\nL50:     Worst-Case Execution Time\nL51: VMs\nL52:     Virtual Machines\nL53: MBR\nL54:     Memory Bandwidth Reser\nL55: Centro ALGORITMI / LASI, Universidade do Minho, Portugaldiogocostaes21@gmail.comhttps://orcid.org/0000-0002-0725-5861Supported by FCT grant 2022.13378.BD Centro ALGORITMI / LASI, Universidade do Minho, Portugalpg53841@alunos.uminho.pthttps://orcid.org/0009-0009-9883-1403 Centro ALGORITMI / LASI, Universidade do Minho, Portugalpg53599@alunos.uminho.pthttps://orcid.org/0009-0007-9996-940X Centro ALGORITMI / LASI, Universidade do Minho, Portugaljose.martins@dei.uminho.pthttps://orcid.org/0000-0001-9380-7150Supported by FCT grant SFRH/BD/138660/2018 Centro ALGORITMI / LASI, Universidade do Minho, Portugalsandro.pinto@dei.uminho.pthttps://orcid.org/0000-0003-4580-7484 \\Copyright Diogo Costa, Gonçalo Moreira, Afonso Oliveira, Jose Martins, Sandro Pinto \\ccsdesc[500]Computer systems organization Real-time system specification \\ccsdesc[500]Computer systems organization Embedded software \\EventEditors Patrick Meumeu Yomsi and Stefan Wildermann \\EventNoEds 2 \\EventLongTitle Sixth Workshop on Next Generation Real-Time Embedded Systems (NG-RES 2025) \\EventShortTitle NG-RES 2025 \\EventAcronym NG-RES \\EventYear 2025 \\EventDate January 20, 2025 \\EventLocation Barcelona, Spain \\EventLogo \\SeriesVolume 128 \\ArticleNo 2\nL56: # SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis\nL57: \nL58: Diogo Costa    Gonçalo Moreira    Afonso Oliveira    José Martins    Sandro Pinto\nL59: ###### Abstract\nL60: Modern embedded systems are evolving toward complex, heterogeneous architectures to accommodate increasingly demanding applications. Driven by industry SWAP-C (Size, Weight, Power, and Cost) constraints, this shift has led to the consolidation of multiple systems onto single hardware platforms. Static Partitioning Hypervisors (SPHs) offer a promising solution to partition hardware resources and provide spatial isolation between critical workloads.\nL61: However, shared hardware resources like the Last-Level Cache (LLC) and system bus can introduce significant temporal interference between virtual machines (VMs), negatively impacting performance and predictability. Over the past decade, academia and industry have focused on developing interference mitigation techniques, such as cache partitioning and memory bandwidth reservation. Configuring these techniques, however, is complex and time-consuming.\nL62: Cache partitioning requires careful balancing of cache sections across VMs, while memory bandwidth reservation requires tuning bandwidth budgets and periods. With numerous possible configurations, testing all combinations is impractical and often leads to suboptimal configurations. Moreover, there is a gap in understanding how these techniques interact, as their combined use can result in compounded or conflicting effects on system performance.\nL63: Static analysis solutions that estimate worst-case execution times (WCET) and upper bounds on execution times provide some guidance for configuring interference mitigation techniques. While useful in identifying potential interference effects, these tools often fail to capture the full complexity of modern multi-core systems, as they typically focus on a limited set of shared resources and neglect other sources of contention, such as IOMMUs and interrupt controllers.\nL64: To address these challenges, we introduce SP-IMPact, an open-source framework designed to analyze and guide the configuration of interference mitigation techniques, through the deployment of diverse VM configurations and setups, and assessment of hardware-level contention (leveraging SPHs). It supports two mitigation techniques: (i) cache coloring and (ii) memory bandwidth reservation, while also evaluating the interactions between these techniques and their cumulative impact on system performance.\nL65: By providing insights on real hardware platforms, SP-IMPact helps to optimize the configuration of these techniques in mixed-criticality systems, ensuring both performance and predictability.\nL66: ###### keywords\nL67: \nL68: Virtualization, Contention, Multi-core Interference, Mixed-Criticality Systems, Arm\nL69: \nL70: ###### category\nL71: \nL72: \\relatedversion\nL73: ## 1 Introduction\nL74: In recent decades, a significant trend toward digitization has revolutionized various industries including automotive, robotics, medical, and aerospace [cite18†9 , cite19†40 , cite20†41 ]. This shift brought an exponential increase in system features, prompting high-end embedded platforms to evolve from basic designs. Past simple MCUs with single cores have given way to today’s intricate and highly complex platforms [cite21†11 ].\nL75: The transition from single-core to multi-core architectures, accommodating multiple CPUs, and integrating diverse hardware accelerators like cite22†GPUs (cite22†GPUs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ), cite23†TPUs (cite23†TPUs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ), cite24†NPUs (cite24†NPUs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ), and cite25†FPGAs (cite25†FPGAs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ) [cite26†18 , cite27†33 , cite28†37 ], has fundamentally altered the landscape, resulting in highly heterogeneous designs.\nL76: Simultaneously, market demands for compact and efficient systems have driven the consolidation of multiple functionalities onto single hardware platforms to meet cite29†SWaP-C (cite29†SWaP-C (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ) constraints.\nL77: This consolidation has led to the rise of cite30†MCSs (cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ) [cite31†21 ], where components with varying criticality levels coexist on the same platform.\nL78: Virtualization technologies have been instrumental in enabling such consolidation, with hypervisor-based solutions—particularly static-partitioning hypervisors [cite32†35 , cite33†34 , cite20†41 , cite34†22 , cite35†27 ]—striking a balance between safety, security, and resource efficiency.\nL79: These hypervisors allow for the deployment of diverse workloads within cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) while adhering to stringent industry safety standards, such as ISO 26262 [cite36†39 ].\nL80: Achieving robust system consolidation requires addressing critical challenges to ensure safety and security, particularly spatial and temporal isolation. Spatial isolation guarantees that architectural resources (e.g., CPUs and main memory) allocated to one system remain inaccessible to others. Temporal isolation ensures that the execution of one system’s workloads does not interfere with another’s timing requirements.\nL81: While static partitioning effectively addresses spatial isolation, temporal isolation remains a significant challenge due to contention on shared microarchitectural resources like the cite37†LLC (cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ), main memory, and system bus.\nL82: Such contention leads to increased execution times and reduced determinism [cite38†1 , cite39†7 , cite40†8 , cite41†31 , cite38†1 ], making timing predictability particularly difficult for hard real-time systems.\nL83: Techniques such as cache partitioning [cite42†17 ] and memory bandwidth reservation [cite43†51 ] have emerged as promising solutions to mitigate temporal interference in cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) .\nL84: Cache partitioning segments the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) into regions assigned to specific cite44†VMs (cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ), while memory bandwidth reservation regulates the number of memory accesses within a given time frame.\nL85: However, configuring these techniques effectively requires careful balancing of resources across cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) and fine-tuning parameters (e.g., define cache regions and/or memory budgets and periods).\nL86: This process is complex, time-consuming, and impractical for real-world cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , often leading to suboptimal configurations.\nL87: Static analysis tools [cite38†1 , cite45†3 ] have been explored to address these challenges, offering a means to understand interference impacts in cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) and guide the configuration of mitigation techniques.\nL88: By estimating cite46†WCET (cite46†WCET (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) ) and quantifying interference effects, these tools provide a foundation for informed decision-making.\nL89: However, existing static analysis solutions often focus on specific shared resources, such as the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , overlooking other shared hardware resources (e.g., IOMMUs and interrupt controllers).\nL90: To address these limitations, we introduce SP-IMPact, an open-source framework ^{1}^{1} 1 https://gitlab.com/ESRGv3/sp-impact designed to analyze and support the configuration of interference mitigation techniques. SP-IMPact enables a comprehensive understanding of the impact of shared hardware resources on real platforms by considering all potential sources of contention - filling critical gaps left by currently available solutions.\nL91: Furthermore, it allows the deployment of diverse configurations of cache coloring and memory bandwidth reservation to evaluate their effects on the workloads of different cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) . The insights gained through SP-IMPact can guide the optimization of these techniques, easing their usage by industry due to the framework’s workload-agnostic design, which supports various operating systems and workloads.\nL92: For academia, SP-IMPact provides a versatile launchpad for deploying and testing new interference mitigation techniques. While this paper leverages the Bao hypervisor [cite33†34 ] as a use case, SP-IMPact is agnostic to the underlying hypervisor and can be extended to support other static partitioning hypervisors.\nL93: ## 2 Background\nL94: The consolidation of cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) introduces a well-known challenge: interference between co-existing cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , which can degrade performance and disrupt real-time guarantees [cite26†18 , cite33†34 , cite47†28 , cite48†50 , cite49†48 , cite43†51 , cite50†4 , cite51†46 , cite52†13 , cite53†30 , cite54†29 ].\nL95: This interference typically arises from contention over shared micro-architectural resources, such as the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , main memory, and the system bus, leading to increased execution time and lack of determinism.\nL96: To mitigate these issues, techniques such as cache partitioning [cite47†28 , cite55†36 , cite56†26 ] and memory bandwidth reservation [cite43†51 , cite48†50 , cite55†36 , cite57†15 ], among others solutions targeting I/O and interrupts regulation [cite58†52 , cite59†16 , cite60†12 ] , have been proposed.\nL97: Cache Partitioning: Cache partitioning techniques enable the selective allocation of cache regions to specific workloads, thereby reducing cache contention and improving predictability.\nL98: One of the most used techniques for cache partitioning is cache coloring, which divides the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) into distinct regions by assigning specific \"colors\" to individual workloads, where each \"color\" corresponds to different cache sets, which helps to control cache access patterns and minimize interference.\nL99: Cache coloring is commonly used to assign dedicated portions of the cache to individual VMs, effectively limiting cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) contention, as depicted in Figure cite61†1 (a).\nL100: Memory Bandwidth Reservation: Memory bandwidth reservation techniques regulate the memory access rate to reduce interference and ensure temporal isolation. MemGuard [cite43†51 ], for example, limits the memory access rate per CPU by allocating specific portions of memory bandwidth, or \"budgets,\" over defined periods, as depicted in Figure cite61†1 (b).\nL101: This prevents any workload from monopolizing memory, ensuring fair resource distribution and reducing delays, thus improving predictable performance and minimizing interference between workloads.\nL102: (a)\nL103: \nL104: (b)\nL105: \nL106: Figure 1: Illustrative examples of cache coloring and memory bandwidth reservation mechanisms.\nL107: ## 3 System Overview\nL108: In this paper, we introduce SP-IMPact, a framework developed to evaluate and benchmark the performance of cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , with a focus on measuring interference and assessing the effectiveness and interaction of interference mitigation techniques. Using a configuration file, users can specify the platform, guest definitions, and test setups.\nL109: As shown in Figure cite62†2 , the framework leverages three key components: (i) the Guest Generator, which build guests; (ii) the Cache Coloring Generator, which defines cache partitioning configurations; and the (iii) Memory Bandwidth Regulation Generator, which generates different configurations of the mechanism. Together, these components ensure precise and consistent performance evaluations.\nL110: SP-IMPact also includes a Logging Monitor for collecting run-time data and an Output Results module to handle the gathered information for further analysis.\nL111: Guests Generator: In the context of evaluating system performance, the framework provides support for constructing different types of guests, each tailored for specific benchmarking or interference generation tasks. For the sake of simplicity, this discussion focuses on two primary guest types: a Linux guest and a baremetal guest. However, it should be noted that the framework can be extended to support more and varied guest types as needed.\nL112: \nL113:   1. 1.\nL114: Linux Benchmark: Designed to simplify the deployment of various Linux-based workloads, enabling the evaluation of system behavior across diverse scenarios.\nL115: \nL116:   2. 2.\nL117: \nL118: Contention Engine: A baremetal guest tailored to create memory and hardware resource pressure, targeting the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) and main memory. Key parameters include CPU count, workload sizes, and operation types (reads, writes, or both).\nL119: cite63†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: SP-IMPact System Overview.\nL120: \nL121: To formalize these configuration options for the baremetal guest, let M represent the total number of CPUs available, L denote the set of possible cache line sizes, W signify the set of workload sizes, and O define the set of operation types, where $\\textbf{{O}}=\\{\\text{read},\\text{write},\\text{read/write}\\}$. The configuration space for the baremetal guest can thus be expressed as:\nL122:  | $$G_{\\text{baremetal}}=\\{(m,l,w,o)\\mid m\\in M,l\\in L,w\\in W,o\\in O\\}$$  |  | (1)\nL123: \nL124: where $G_{\\text{baremetal}}$ represents the set of all possible configurations for the baremetal guest. To represent the complete configuration space for all guests, including both baremetal and Linux guests, let $G$ denote the overall set of guest configurations, which is equal to $G_{\\text{baremetal}}\\cup G_{\\text{linux}}$, where $G_{\\text{linux}}$ represents the set of configurations for the Linux guest.\nL125: Cache Coloring Generator: In cite30†MCSs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) , cache partitioning is crucial for reducing cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) interference and ensuring predictable performance. This process, tipicaly based on cache coloring, aims to divide cache sets into non-overlapping regions for each VM.\nL126: To assess the impact of different cache partitions, the BaoRTI framework supports the generation of distinct cache color configurations based on the following parameters:\nL127:   1. 1.\nL128: \nL129: The total number of cache sets S, which corresponds to the bit length of the bitmap used to define the color assignments (i.e., each cache set index is represented as a bit in the range $[0,S-1]$);\nL130: \nL131:   2. 2.\nL132: \nL133: The number of VMs, N, to which distinct cache partitions will be assigned.\nL134: The objective of the function is to generate unique configurations of bit masks, dividing the bit range $[0,S-1]$ into N distinct non-overlapping sections. Each section represents a cache partition that can be assigned as a color to the VMs. Given S cache sets and N VMs, the function produces a unique configuration of non-overlapping bit masks for each VM. To facilitate this process, we denote C as the set of all possible $(N-1)$-combinations of bit positions within the range $[0,S-1]$.\nL135: Each combination in C represents potential VM coloring configurations and can be formally defined as:\nL136:  | $$\\textit{{C}}=\\{(b_{1},b_{2},\\dots,b_{N-1})\\mid 0\\leq b_{1}<b_{2}<\\dots<b_{N-1}<S\\}$$  |  | (2)\nL137: \nL138: where each $b_{i}$ denotes a bit position that separates the partitions for each VM.\nL139: To generate the bit masks for each VM, we begin by initializing the starting bit $s_{0}$ to $0$. For each VM $i$, where $i$ ranges from $0$ to $N-1$, we define the end bit $e_{i}$ based on the boundary positions: if $i<N-1$, $e_{i}$ is set to $b_{i}$, whereas for the last VM ($i=N-1$), $e_{i}$ is assigned the total number of cache sets $S$. With these boundaries established, we compute the bit mask $M_{i}$ for each VM using the formula:\nL140: \nL141:  | $$M_{i}=((1\\ll(e_{i}-s_{i}))-1)\\ll s_{i}$$  |  | (3)\nL142: where \"$\\ll$\" represents a left bit shift operation. This formula creates a mask with $(e_{i}-s_{i})$ bits set to 1, aligned to begin at the position defined by $s_{i}$. After calculating the bit mask, we update the starting bit $s_{i}$ for the next VM by setting it to the current end bit $e_{i}$. This iterative process continues until all masks for the VMs are generated, ensuring that each VM receives a unique configuration of non-overlapping cache partitions.\nL143: After generating a list of bit masks for each VM in the current combination, this configuration is added to a result set colors_assignments if it does not already exist in the set. This ensures that all configurations in that list are unique.\nL144: Memory Bandwidth Regulation Generator: In real-time systems, generating distinct memory bandwidth configurations for cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) is essential for ensuring predictable performance and efficient resource utilization. This process, known as memory bandwidth reservation, focuses on creating unique combinations of budget and sampling period for each VM.\nL145: To evaluate the effects of various bandwidth configurations, the BaoRTI framework supports the generation of distinct MBR configurations based on the following parameters:\nL146:   1. 1.\nL147: \nL148: A list of budgets B available for reservation, where each budget specifies the maximum amount of bandwidth allocated to a VM.\nL149: \nL150:   2. 2.\nL151: \nL152: A list of sampling periods P, which define the time intervals at which the allocated bandwidth should be monitored.\nL153: The objective of the memory bandwidth reservation assignment generation is to produce all the possible combinations of budget and period assignments for each guest. Given the set of all guests, G, where each guest, g, is associated with a set of budgets, $B_{g}$, and a set of periods, $P_{g}$, the configuration for each guest can be expressed as:\nL154: \nL155:  | $$MBR_{g}=\\{(B,P)\\mid B\\in B_{g},P\\in P_{g}\\}$$  |  | (4)\nL156: where $MBR_{g}$ represents the set of all combinations of memory bandwidth reservation configurations for the guest $g$. Let $G$ be the total number of guests, $B$ be the maximum number of budgets across guests, and $P$ be the maximum number of sampling periods across guests. The time complexity for generating the budget-period combinations for each guest is $O(B\\times P)$.\nL157: Since there are $G$ guests, the overall complexity for processing all guests and generating their combinations can be expressed as $O\\left(G\\times B\\times P\\right)$.\nL158: SP-IMPact features a results logging system designed to capture essential performance and behavioral metrics from the target platform during test execution. The framework collects data from multiple serial ports, each mapped to a specific \\ac{VM}, ensuring comprehensive monitoring across the system. The captured metrics include execution time and key micro-architectural events, such as acLLC misses, memory access counts, and cycles spent on the system bus.\nL159: These metrics are vital for evaluating the impact of shared hardware resources—like the cite37†LLC (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) and memory controllers—on workload performance and predictability. This versatile design enables SP-IMPact to support a wide array of benchmarks and metrics tailored to diverse interference scenarios.\nL160: By correlating data across multiple cite44†VMs (SP-IMPact: A Framework for Static Partitioning Interference Mitigation and Performance Analysis) and configurations, SP-IMPact provides the granularity required to assess the effectiveness of interference mitigation techniques and optimize their configurations.\nL161: ## 4 Evaluation\nL162: ### 4.1 Evaluation Setup\nL163: Hardware Platform. The experiments were conducted on a Xilinx ZCU104 evaluation board equipped with a Zynq Ultrascale+ ZU7EV SoC. This platform includes a quad-core Arm Cortex-A53 processor, operating at 1.2 GHz. While the SoC supports up to 16 distinct cache colors for cache coloring, the Bao hypervisor constrains this to 8 colors to avoid partitioning the L1 cache. Each core has a dedicated 32 KiB L1 instruction and data cache, along with a unified 1 MiB L2 cache.\nL164: Additionally, the board is equipped with an Arm Performance Monitoring Unit (PMU), which was leveraged to collect microarchitectural events (such as cache misses and system bus accesses) and profile the benchmark.\nL165: Workloads. For our evaluation, we leveraged the MiBench Automotive and Industrial Control System (AICS) [cite64†19 ] Suite within the critical VM. This subset includes three memory-intensive benchmarks: qsort, susan-c, and susan-e. To generate interference at the memory hierarchy, we deployed a baremetal application that continuously performs read or write operations on a buffer with different sizes.\nL166: Specifically, buffer sizes include 32 KiB (100% of the L1 cache), 512 KiB (50% of the L2 cache), 1 MiB (100% of the L2 cache), 1.5 MiB (150% of the L2 cache), 2 MiB (200% of the L2 cache), and 4 MiB (400% of the L2 cache).\nL167: Setups. According to Equation cite65†1 , we consider the following parameters: $L=1$ (using only the cache line size matching the cache line size of the target hardware platform), $C=1$ (using only one CPU configuration, which assigns 3 CPUs to the baremetal VM), $W=6$ (the total number of workloads used), and $O=2$ (representing both read and write operations). This results in a total of 12 variations of the baremetal guest.\nL168: Additionally, for cache coloring, since there are 2 VMs ($N=2$) and 8 possible cache sets ($S=8$), there are 8 unique configurations of cache coloring. However, we excluded scenarios in which the Linux VM would be allocated only a single cache color, as such configurations would not provide meaningful performance benefits. Thus, combining these factors results in a total of 84 setups to be tested.\nL169: For simplicity, we will not consider the configuration of MBR, as introducing it would significantly increase the total number of setups in this evaluation section.\nL170: Setup Naming Convention. Setups are named solo or interf_<access>_<buffer_size>. The solo setup serves as the baseline, where a Linux VM runs the MiBench benchmarks without interference. In interf_<access>_<buffer size> setups, an additional workload creates cache contention, with access specifying read or write interference type and buffer_size indicating the buffer size used. Cache coloring setups add the suffix <cc_num-colors>, where num-colors denotes the cache colors allocated to the critical VM.\nL171: ### 4.2 Interference Impact on Multi-core Platforms\nL172: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShareMMU: Supporting Secure Address Translation Sharing among Untrusted Accelerators | Research (https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2026-09_sharemmu-supporting-secure-address-translation-sharing-among-untrusted)\nciteturn65view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn64view0\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 115\nL0: Research Labs\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†All Research Labs L3:   * cite1†Spatial Intelligence L4:   * cite2†Applied Research L5:   * cite3†Autonomous Systems and Physical AI L6:   * cite4†NVIDIA Cosmos Lab L7: \nL8: cite5†Publications L9: \nL10: AI Playground\nL11: \nL12:   * cite6†New and Featured†www.nvidia.com L13:   * cite7†AI Art Gallery†www.nvidia.com L14:   * cite8†NGC Demos†catalog.ngc.nvidia.com L15: \nL16: Research Areas\nL17: \nL18:   * cite9†AI & Machine Learning L19:   * cite1†3D Deep Learning L20:   * cite10†Computer Vision L21:   * cite11†Robotics L22:   * cite12†All Areas†www.nvidia.com L23: \nL24: Careers\nL25:   * cite13†Academic Collaborations†www.nvidia.com L26:   * cite14†Government Collaborations†www.nvidia.com L27:   * cite15†Graduate Fellowship L28:   * cite16†Internships†www.nvidia.com L29:   * cite17†Research Openings†nvidia.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com L30:   * cite18†Research Scientists†www.nvidia.com L31:   * cite19†Meet the Team L32: \nL33: cite20†Licensing†www.nvidia.com L34: \nL35:   *   *   * Research Labs\nL36: \nL37:   * cite0†All Research Labs L38:   * cite1†Spatial Intelligence L39:   * cite2†Applied Research L40:   * cite3†Autonomous Systems and Physical AI L41:   * cite4†NVIDIA Cosmos Lab L42: cite5†Publications L43: \nL44: AI Playground\nL45: \nL46:   * cite6†New and Featured†www.nvidia.com L47:   * cite7†AI Art Gallery†www.nvidia.com L48:   * cite8†NGC Demos†catalog.ngc.nvidia.com L49: \nL50: Research Areas\nL51: \nL52:   * cite9†AI & Machine Learning L53:   * cite1†3D Deep Learning L54:   * cite10†Computer Vision L55:   * cite11†Robotics L56:   * cite12†All Areas†www.nvidia.com L57: \nL58: Careers\nL59:   * cite13†Academic Collaborations†www.nvidia.com L60:   * cite14†Government Collaborations†www.nvidia.com L61:   * cite15†Graduate Fellowship L62:   * cite16†Internships†www.nvidia.com L63:   * cite17†Research Openings†nvidia.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com L64:   * cite18†Research Scientists†www.nvidia.com L65:   * cite19†Meet the Team L66: \nL67: cite20†Licensing†www.nvidia.com L68: \nL69: Search\nL70: \nL71: [Input: Search][Button: Search]\nL72: \nL73: Enter the terms you wish to search for.\nL74: # ShareMMU: Supporting Secure Address Translation Sharing among Untrusted Accelerators\nL75: \nL76: cite21†Image L77: The growing demand for accelerated computing is driving widespread deployment of multi-accelerator systems in the cloud and at the edge. These systems are temporally shared across processes that may not mutually trust one another and often include large pools of third-party, untrusted accelerators operating within a shared virtual address space.\nL78: Due to the limited capability of the Input-Output Memory Management Unit (IOMMU) in supporting address translation, SoC designers are integrating large Shared Translation Lookaside Buffers (TLBs) that serve many accelerators and processes. While effective for performance, shared TLBs introduce new security risks that require urgent and careful consideration.\nL79: This paper presents ShareMMU, an IOMMU design that securely enables reuse of pre-translated addresses across multiple untrusted accelerators while also mitigating potential side channels in the shared TLB. Compared to state-of-the-art mechanisms like Border Control and CryptoMMU, which incur 32% and 17% performance overhead, respectively, ShareMMU incurs a negligible 1.66% performance overhead relative to an unsecured baseline.\nL80: Our side-channel resilient variant, ShareMMU-SP, incurs roughly 2.82% overhead.\nL81: ## Authors\nL82: \nL83: Faiz Alam (Granular Labs)\nL84: \nL85: cite22†Mohamed Tarek Ibn Ziad L86: \nL87: Yuanchao Xu (University of California, Santa Cruz)\nL88: \nL89: Abdulrahman Mahmoud (MBZUAI)\nL90: \nL91: Greg Byrd (North Carolina State University)\nL92: \nL93: cite23†Aamer Jaleel L94: \nL95: ## Publication Date\nL96: \nL97: Tuesday, September 15, 2026\nL98: \nL99: ## Published in\nL100: \nL101: cite24†59th IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO)†www.microarch.org L102: \nL103: ## Research Area\nL104: \nL105: cite25†Computer Architecture L106: \nL107: cite26†Security L108: ## Uploaded Files\nL109: \nL110: cite27†ShareMMU_MICRO26_Camera_Ready.pdf†d1qx31qr3h6wln.cloudfront.net 961.81 KB\nL111: \nL112: ## Copyright\nL113: \nL114: This material is posted here with permission of the IEEE. Internal or personal use of this material is permitted. However, permission to reprint/republish this material for advertising or promotional purposes or for creating new collective works for resale or redistribution must be obtained from the IEEE by writing to pubs-permissions@ieee.org.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn65view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://parallel.princeton.edu/papers/ISCA20-hypertrio.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"